window.__hvBdRegister("plut-moralia-2", {"key":"plut-moralia-2","sections":[{"i":0,"j":56,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 1–21","title":"Dedication to Trajan; Persian, barbarian and Sicilian rulers","summary":"Plutarch dedicates the collection to the emperor Trajan, asking him to accept a small gift as Artaxerxes accepted a peasant's handful of water, and explains that sayings reveal character more clearly than deeds, which are mixed with fortune. Sayings follow from Persian kings (Cyrus, Darius, Xerxes, the Artaxerxes), from Thracian and Scythian rulers such as Cotys, Ateas and Scilurus with his bundle of darts, and from the Sicilian tyrants Gelon and Hieron. The section ends with a long run of sayings of Dionysius the Elder, showing his cunning and his hard view of tyranny.","ideas":["Words show a man's disposition better than actions governed by fortune","Generous rule: Darius halving a tax, Artaxerxes paying a petitioner rather than granting an unjust request","Scilurus teaches his sons that unity is strength with a bundle of darts","Dionysius the Elder treats tyranny as something kept by craft and suspicion"],"terms":[{"t":"apophthegma (saying)","d":"A short memorable remark or reply, collected here as a mirror of each man's character."},{"t":"tyrannos (tyrant)","d":"A sole ruler holding power outside traditional law, like Gelon, Hieron and the Dionysii of Syracuse."}]},{"i":57,"j":102,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 22–26","title":"Dionysius the Younger, Agathocles, Archelaus and Philip","summary":"Dionysius the Younger says philosophy taught him to bear the loss of his power, and that he inherited his father's rule but not his fortune. Agathocles the potter's son shows earthen and golden vessels side by side, Dion refuses to question a suspected friend, and Archelaus of Macedon answers a chattering barber. Most of the section gives Philip of Macedon, whom Theophrastus calls freer from luxury than other kings: he tolerates abuse, prefers being called merciful to being called lord, mocks the Athenians' ten generals a year, and accepts correction from an old woman who tells him not to reign if he has no leisure.","ideas":["Dionysius the Younger: philosophy's gift is patience under a change of fortune","Philip holds that whether one is well or ill spoken of lies in one's own power","Philip's restraint toward Athens, the 'theatre of glory'","A ruler's duty to hear petitions: 'Do not reign then'"],"terms":[{"t":"tychē (fortune)","d":"Chance or luck, which these rulers repeatedly contrast with their own merit and conduct."}]},{"i":103,"j":137,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 27","title":"Sayings of Alexander the Great","summary":"As a boy Alexander fears his father will leave him nothing to do and will run at Olympia only against kings. As king he gives lavishly to friends and philosophers, answers Darius that Asia cannot have two kings, tells his men to shave so the enemy cannot seize their beards, and laughs at his own blood as not the ichor of the gods. He shows respect for Porus, the Indian archer, Taxiles and a free woman he will not force, and distinguishes Craterus, friend of the king, from Hephaestion, friend of Alexander. The section closes with his dying remark about his funeral games and Demades likening his leaderless army to the blinded Cyclops.","ideas":["Ambition for deeds rather than inherited possessions","Magnificence in giving: 'enough for you to receive, not for me to give'","Alexander disclaims divinity when wounded","Rivalry in generosity with Taxiles and royal treatment of Porus"],"terms":[{"t":"megalopsychia (greatness of soul)","d":"Lofty generosity and ambition, the quality these anecdotes repeatedly display in Alexander."}]},{"i":138,"j":184,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 28–38","title":"The successors of Alexander and Hellenistic kings","summary":"Ptolemy son of Lagus thinks it kinglier to enrich others than to be rich, and Antigonus is shown at length: gleaning where Alexander reaped, mild in old age for the sake of goodwill, and refusing flattery that calls him son of the Sun. Demetrius spares Protogenes' painting at Rhodes, Antigonus the Second offers himself as hostage for his father, Lysimachus regrets selling a kingdom for a drink, and Antipater, Antiochus the Third and Antiochus Hierax appear briefly. Eumenes forgives the brother who seized his crown and wife, and the section ends with Pyrrhus and an Antiochus who hears the truth about himself only in a peasant's cottage.","ideas":["Kings measure honour by justice, not by flattery","Antigonus: power sought in youth, glory and goodwill in old age","Fraternal loyalty between Eumenes and Attalus","The 'Pyrrhic' remark: another such victory would undo him"],"terms":[{"t":"Diadochi (successors)","d":"The generals and kings who divided Alexander's empire, such as Ptolemy, Antigonus and Lysimachus."}]},{"i":185,"j":263,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 39–52","title":"Athenian statesmen and generals from Themistocles to Phocion","summary":"Themistocles cannot sleep for Miltiades' trophy, tells Eurybiades 'strike, but hear me', tricks Xerxes before and after Salamis, and later says he would have been ruined had he not been ruined. Aristeides writes his own name on an ostracism shell, Pericles reminds himself that he commands free Athenians, and Alcibiades bites 'like a lion' and flees trial. Iphicrates, Timotheus and Chabrias speak as soldiers, and Phocion, the longest entry, opposes the crowd, refuses gifts from Alexander and Antipater, and dies by hemlock forbidding revenge. Peisistratus' forbearance and Demetrius of Phalerum's advice to read books on kingship close the section.","ideas":["Themistocles' stratagems at Salamis and his ambition","Aristeides the Just and ostracism","Phocion's distrust of popular approval and incorruptibility","Merit against birth: Iphicrates' nobility 'begins in me'"],"terms":[{"t":"ostrakismos (ostracism)","d":"Athenian vote, written on potsherds, that sent a citizen into exile for ten years."},{"t":"stratēgos (general)","d":"An elected Athenian military commander, also a leading political office."}]},{"i":264,"j":342,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 53–72","title":"Spartan kings, Epaminondas and Pelopidas","summary":"A short Spartan series gives Lycurgus on long hair and simple houses, then Charillus, Teleclus, Theopompus, Brasidas, Agis, Lysander and Agesilaus, who says no king is greater than he unless more just and wise and asks that no statue be made of him. Then Epaminondas: he keeps watch while the city feasts, refuses Jason's and the Persian king's gold, mourns his own excess of joy after Leuctra, and at his trial asks that his deeds in Laconia be written on his tomb, so that the judges laugh and refuse to vote. Pelopidas, imprisoned by Alexander of Pherae, says he has learned he can die as well as fight.","ideas":["Spartan laconic wit and contempt for walls and luxury","Agesilaus: justice makes valour unnecessary","Epaminondas' frugality, incorruptibility and self-discipline","Pelopidas defies a tyrant in chains"],"terms":[{"t":"Boeotarch","d":"One of the chief magistrates and generals of the Boeotian League, the office Epaminondas held beyond its legal term."}]},{"i":343,"j":410,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 73–81 (Roman Sayings)","title":"Roman sayings: Curius to Cato the Elder","summary":"The Roman part opens with Manius Curius boiling turnips while refusing Samnite gold, and Fabricius, unmoved by Pyrrhus' gold or elephant, who reveals the physician's poison plot. Fabius Maximus delays against Hannibal despite mockery, Scipio the Elder takes Carthage and faces his accusers by going to the Capitol, Titus Quinctius frees Greece, and Paulus Aemilius defeats Perseus and bears the death of his two sons as fortune's price. The section ends with a long series of Cato the Elder's maxims against luxury and on self-government, justice and the censorship.","ideas":["Old Roman frugality and contempt for bribes","Fabius' strategy of delay, vindicated by events","Paulus Aemilius accepts private grief as payment for public success","Cato: the worst governor cannot govern himself"],"terms":[{"t":"censor","d":"Roman magistrate who reviewed citizens' rolls and morals; Cato and Scipio the Younger both held the office."},{"t":"consul","d":"One of Rome's two annually elected chief magistrates and commanders."}]},{"i":411,"j":468,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 82–89","title":"Scipio the Younger, Marius, Lucullus and Pompey","summary":"Scipio the Younger lives without buying or building, returns Sicilian treasures from Carthage, embarrasses the Alexandrians' lazy king, and restores discipline at Numantia by stripping the army of luxury. Caecilius Metellus, Marius, Lutatius Catulus, Sulla, Popilius with his circle in the sand, and Lucullus against Tigranes follow. Pompey forms the longest entry: he wins his triumph from Sulla, tells the censors he served under himself, burns Sertorius' letters, sails in a storm because sailing is necessary and living is not, and dies covering his head after quoting Sophocles on a tyrant's door.","ideas":["Scipio's reform of a corrupted army at Numantia","Popilius forces Antiochus to answer within a drawn circle","Pompey: 'more worship the rising than the setting sun'","Pompey's death on landing in Egypt"],"terms":[{"t":"triumph","d":"Roman victory procession granted by the senate to a successful general."},{"t":"imperator","d":"Title by which soldiers hailed a victorious commander."}]},{"i":469,"j":518,"loc":"Sayings of Kings and Commanders, Reg. apophth. 90–92","title":"Sayings of Cicero, Julius Caesar and Augustus","summary":"Cicero keeps his name and engraves a chick-pea on a cup, and his jokes at Verres, Metellus Nepos, Vatinius and others show his sharp tongue; in the civil war he knows whom to flee but not to whom. Caesar promises to crucify the pirates who held him and does, says Caesar's wife must be above suspicion, crosses the Rubicon, writes 'I came, I saw, I conquered', and wishes for an unexpected death. Augustus pays Julius Caesar's legacies from his own estate, spares Alexandria, takes Athenodorus' advice to recite the alphabet when angry, and ends by praising Piso for building as if Rome were eternal.","ideas":["Cicero's wit as a political weapon","Caesar's daring and decisiveness","Augustus' self-restraint and his care for ordering the empire rather than enlarging it"],"terms":[]},{"i":519,"j":561,"loc":"Apophth. Lac. 1.1–2.38","title":"Agasicles; Agesilaus at home and in Asia","summary":"The Sayings of Spartans open with two sayings of King Agasicles, who would learn only from men he would gladly have as fathers and held that a king rules safely by ruling as a father rules sons. The collection of Agesilaus the Great follows: sayings on justice, keeping one's word only when it is right, honour coming from men rather than places, and refusing elaborate medicine. It then follows his campaign in Asia: consulting Dodona and Delphi, outwitting Tissaphernes, raising cavalry at Ephesus, refusing the Persian king's money, and mastering his desire for Megabates. Sayings on Spartan austerity, refused divine honours from the Thasians, and Sparta's walls being its men lead up to his decision to march against the king himself.","ideas":["Endurance and skill are praised only when put to honourable use","A commander should surpass his men in hardship, not luxury","Self-mastery is valued above conquest and above kingship","Sparta's walls and borders are its armed citizens"],"terms":[{"t":"Ephors","d":"The Spartan magistrates who sent Agesilaus out, checked his conduct, and later recalled him."},{"t":"Helots","d":"Sparta's serf population, to whom Agesilaus gave the Thasians' luxurious foods."}]},{"i":562,"j":582,"loc":"Apophth. Lac. 2.39–2.59","title":"Agesilaus recalled: the march home and Coroneia","summary":"Recalled by the Ephors when Persian gold stirred Greek states to war against Sparta, Agesilaus obeys, joking that thirty thousand 'bowmen' (Persian coins) drove him out of Asia, and writes that a ruler rules rightly by following the laws. On the march through Thrace he fights the Trallians, presses past the Macedonian king, recovers his envoys from Larissa, and grieves over Greek dead at Corinth. He defeats the Pharsalian cavalry, wins at Coroneia despite many wounds, and returns home unchanged in his way of life. Further sayings show him using Cynisca's chariot victory to deflate horse-breeders, burying Lysander's revolutionary speech, winning over opponents, and snubbing the actor Callippides and the physician Menecrates who called himself Zeus.","ideas":["A good commander is himself subject to the laws","Victory over fellow Greeks is a cause for grief, not joy","Racing studs show wealth, not excellence","Spartan education teaches how to rule and be ruled"],"terms":[{"t":"Coroneia","d":"The battle in Boeotia that Agesilaus won against a coalition of Greek cities, called by Xenophon the greatest of his day."}]},{"i":583,"j":602,"loc":"Apophth. Lac. 2.60–2.79","title":"Agesilaus: Thebes, Leuctra, Egypt and death","summary":"The collection clears Agesilaus of the peace of Antalcidas that surrendered the Asian Greeks, then gathers sayings that justice outranks bravery and that the Persian king is not greater unless more just. Antalcidas reproaches him for training the Thebans in war by constant campaigns, and Agesilaus shows the allies that only Spartans are full-time soldiers. After Leuctra he restores the laws 'from the morrow' to spare the runaways, holds Sparta against Epameinondas, and at Mantineia urges the Spartans to strike at Epameinondas alone. In Egypt he is despised for his plain dress, uses a trick of letters printed on a sacrificial liver to embolden his men, and defeats an encircling enemy; dying on the way home, he forbids any statue of himself.","ideas":["Bravery is useless without justice","Laws can be suspended for a day without being abolished","Greatness comes from understanding and virtue, not apparel","A man's deeds, not statues, are his memorial"],"terms":[{"t":"Leuctra","d":"The Spartan defeat by Thebes after which the law disgracing those who fled raised a crisis of manpower."}]},{"i":603,"j":632,"loc":"Apophth. Lac. 3.1–7.1","title":"Sayings of Agesipolis and the kings Agis","summary":"Agesipolis son of Cleombrotus remarks on Philip's razing of Olynthus and accepts being a hostage himself, since men should bear their own mistakes; Agesipolis son of Pausanias shames the Athenians for seeking Megara as arbiter. Agis son of Archidamus refuses to trust Spartan youths to a traitor and offers terse sayings: Spartans ask where the enemy are, not how many, high walls are for women, and long-winded envoys get silence. He mocks the Eleans' four-yearly justice and laughs at the old complaint that things are getting worse. The younger Agis answers Demades on short swords, and the last Agis, condemned without trial, goes to the noose saying he is better than his killers.","ideas":["Brevity of speech and contempt for death mark the free man","Those in authority should bear the consequences of their errors","Complaints of decline are repeated in every generation"],"terms":[{"t":"Laconic speech","d":"The terse, pointed Spartan style of reply illustrated throughout, especially in Agis's answers to envoys."}]},{"i":633,"j":684,"loc":"Apophth. Lac. 8.1–19.8","title":"Acrotatus to Archidamus son of Zeuxidamus","summary":"Acrotatus declines to join his parents in an injustice, saying the country's laws and his training in justice must come first. Alcamenes, Anaxandridas, Anaxander, Anaxilas and Androcleidas give short sayings on refusing gifts, exile from justice, care of oneself rather than one's fields, slow judgement in capital cases, and an unguarded treasury. Antalcidas trades retorts with Athenians and repeats his reproach to Agesilaus over the Thebans; Antiochus, Areus, Ariston and Archidamidas add sayings on Philip, praise of other men's wives, making friends of enemies, and timely silence. Archidamus son of Zeuxidamus closes the range: the laws rule Sparta, musicians rank with soup-makers, Dionysius's robes are refused, and his rash son should match courage to strength.","ideas":["Duty to country and justice outranks obedience to parents","Doing good to friends and making friends of enemies","Pleasures of music and fine food are of one kind","Spartan young men are the city's walls"],"terms":[{"t":"Athena of the Brazen House","d":"Sparta's shrine of Athena, where Archidamus made two disputants swear to settle their quarrel."}]},{"i":685,"j":773,"loc":"Sayings of Spartans, Apophth. Lac. 20–44","title":"Spartan sayings: Archidamus son of Agesilaus to Callicratidas","summary":"Archidamus son of Agesilaus tells Philip to measure his shadow, writes to the Eleans that quiet is good, and laments the catapult as the end of valour. Brasidas lets go the mouse that bites him, and his mother says Sparta has many better men. Demaratus, Eudamidas (who mocks the aged Xenocrates for still seeking virtue), Theopompus, Themisteas the prophet at Thermopylae and the old Hippodamus add sayings on law, speech and duty. Callicratidas the admiral refuses bribes, waits in vain on Cyrus rather than demean Sparta, and fights at Arginusae despite an omen of his death, saying Sparta's fate rests on no one man.","ideas":["Courage defends even the small (Brasidas' mouse)","Contempt for philosophy that is not put to use","The shield carried for the whole line, not oneself","Callicratidas chooses death over flight or servility"],"terms":[{"t":"lakōnismos (laconic speech)","d":"The terse, pointed Spartan manner of speaking that these replies illustrate."}]},{"i":774,"j":827,"loc":"Sayings of Spartans, Apophth. Lac. 45–52","title":"Cleomenes, Leotychidas and Leonidas at Thermopylae","summary":"Cleomenes son of Anaxandridas calls Homer the poet of Spartans and Hesiod of Helots; he breaks a truce with Argos by attacking at night, is repelled by the women of Argos, and later dies by his own hand in madness. Leotychidas mocks an omen and an Orphic priest who promises bliss after death, and Leo describes the safest city. The section closes with Leonidas: he tells Gorgo to marry good men, answers Xerxes 'come and take them', welcomes fighting in the shade of Persian arrows, and tries in vain to send three grown men home before the battle.","ideas":["Cleomenes' treachery and its ruin, told without approval","Contempt for omens and promises of bliss after death","Leonidas' choice to die for Greece rather than rule it"],"terms":[{"t":"Thermopylae","d":"The pass where Leonidas and his men held off Xerxes' army in 480 BC."}]},{"i":828,"j":859,"loc":"Apophth. Lac. 53.1–53.31","title":"Sayings and ordinances of Lycurgus the lawgiver","summary":"Lycurgus shows with two dogs that training outweighs birth, then redistributes land equally, abolishes debts, and replaces gold and silver with heavy iron money, which drives out theft, bribery, and luxury trades. He institutes the common meals, survives an attack in which Alcander puts out his eye and then wins Alcander over, and explains why he left no written laws. Many sayings give his reasons: plain houses, no frequent wars against one foe, athletic training and processions for girls, marriage without dowries, timing and secrecy of marriage, small sacrifices, and no assaults on walled places. Geradatas's joke about the bull that drinks from the Eurotas shows that adultery was unknown; the section ends with his rules to spare those who flee and not to strip the dead.","ideas":["Training is more effective than nature for good","Equality of land, meals and money removes envy and crime","Women's strength and virtue are trained like men's","A city fortified by men needs no walls"],"terms":[{"t":"Common meals (syssitia)","d":"Shared public dining Lycurgus instituted so that rich and poor ate alike."},{"t":"Iron money","d":"Heavy, nearly worthless coinage that made wealth useless to hide, steal or display."}]},{"i":860,"j":933,"loc":"Sayings of Spartans, Apophth. Lac. 54–68","title":"Lysander, Pausanias and other Spartan kings","summary":"Lysander is shown as cunning: he pieces out the lion's skin with the fox's, says men are tricked with oaths, and is found after death to have written a plan to make the kingship elective, which Cratidas persuades Agesilaus to bury with him; his poverty then shames his daughters' suitors. Pausanias son of Cleombrotus mocks Persian luxury after Plataea, and Pausanias son of Pleistoanax insists laws must rule men and jokes at physicians. Paedaretus rejoices at not being chosen among the Three Hundred, and Polydorus, Teleclus and Charillus explain Spartan customs, ending with Charillus on armed statues of the gods and long hair.","ideas":["Lysander's view of justice as expediency, which the text reports critically","Lysander's secret scheme to open the kingship to the best men","Laws should have authority over men, not men over laws","Honour as cheerfully yielding to better citizens"],"terms":[{"t":"Eurypontids and Agiads","d":"The two royal houses of Sparta, both claiming descent from Heracles."},{"t":"the Three Hundred","d":"A select body of young Spartans, membership in which counted as the highest honour."}]},{"i":934,"j":1019,"loc":"Sayings of Spartans, Apophth. Lac. 69","title":"Sayings of Spartans whose names are unknown","summary":"Anonymous Spartans mock long speeches, Athenian pride and painted victories, and answer Philip and Antipater with refusals and readiness to die. Stories of the boys follow: the boy who lets a hidden fox eat into his side rather than be caught, and the captive who throws himself from a roof rather than fetch a chamber-pot. Bulis and Sperchis offer themselves to Xerxes and refuse his honours for liberty, and at Olympia only the Spartans rise for an old man, who says all Greeks know what is right but Spartans alone practise it. The collection ends with jests on ghosts, vows and obeying the recall.","ideas":["Freedom valued above life, even by captives and boys","Respect for the old as distinctively Spartan","Obedience to the commander over killing an enemy","Wit against wordiness, luxury and boasting"],"terms":[{"t":"agōgē (Spartan training)","d":"The Spartan upbringing of boys, including the custom of stealing without being caught, alluded to here."}]},{"i":1020,"j":1075,"loc":"The Ancient Customs of the Spartans 1–42 (236–240)","title":"Spartan customs under the laws of Lycurgus","summary":"A list of forty-two Spartan practices: secrecy at the public meals, the black broth, walking home without torches, schooling limited to letters, one garment a year, beds of Eurotas reeds, and the elders' authority to question and rebuke any young man. Boys steal their food and are kept lean to make them hardy; music is kept simple, with three choirs of old men, men and boys, and the Ephors cut the extra strings of Terpander and Timotheus. Lycurgus removes fear of the dead, bans foreign travel and foreigners, shares slaves, dogs and stores in common, and frees citizens from trades for a life of leisure worked by Helots. The list closes with a historical sketch: Sparta led Greece for five hundred years while it kept these laws, declined as greed for money came in, and has now, like the rest of Greece, come under Rome.","ideas":["All education aims at obedience, endurance and victory or death in battle","Every elder has authority over every youth, so that much is held in common","Music and drill are joined so that warlike spirit is kept in harmony","Sparta's greatness rises and falls with keeping the laws of Lycurgus"],"terms":[{"t":"Ephors","d":"Spartan magistrates who enforce the old rules, here even on musicians' instruments."},{"t":"Helots","d":"The state serfs who till Spartan land for a fixed return, leaving citizens free of labour."}]},{"i":1076,"j":1129,"loc":"Sayings of Spartan Women (240–242)","title":"Sayings of Spartan mothers, wives and captives","summary":"Short sayings grouped by named women and then by anonymous ones. Argileonis says Sparta has better men than her dead son Brasidas; Gorgo warns her father Cleomenes against the Milesian Aristagoras and tells an Attic woman that Spartan women rule men because they bear men; Gyrtias and Damatria judge their sons by their courage. Anonymous mothers kill or disown sons who flee, ask first how the country fares, rejoice in sons who die well, and send sons out with the shield, telling them to come back with it or on it. The section closes with sayings on chastity and with captive women sold as slaves who answer that they know how to be faithful, to manage a house, or to be free.","ideas":["A son's worth is measured wholly by his service to Sparta","Mothers rank the city's victory above their own children's lives","Even in captivity Spartan women hold to freedom and self-respect"],"terms":[{"t":"Laconic speech","d":"The terse, pointed style of reply that every saying here shows."}]},{"i":1130,"j":1155,"loc":"Bravery of Women, preface and 1–4 (242–245)","title":"Men's and women's virtue; first collective deeds","summary":"Addressing Clea after the death of Leontis, Plutarch rejects Thucydides' view that the best woman is least talked of, and argues that the virtue of men and of women is one and the same. As painting or poetry is one art whoever practises it, so virtue is best shown by setting lives and actions side by side; virtues take on different colourings from different natures without becoming different kinds. He then begins with deeds done by women together: the Trojan women burn their ships in Italy, the Phocian women vote for their own burning if their men are beaten, the Chian women teach their men to keep their arms and later repel Philip, and the Argive women under the poet Telesilla drive off Cleomenes.","ideas":["Virtue is one in men and women, though its colouring varies by temperament","Comparing lives and deeds is the proper way to show this","Several customs and festivals are traced to women's acts of courage"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"Excellence of character, which Plutarch holds to be the same in both sexes."},{"t":"Desperation","d":"The Greek name for the Phocian vote to burn women and children rather than let them be enslaved."}]},{"i":1156,"j":1178,"loc":"Bravery of Women 5–13 (246–249)","title":"Women who shamed, saved or restrained their peoples","summary":"Persian women shame their fleeing men into rallying against the Medes, and Celtic women end a civil war and are made arbiters even in the treaty with Hannibal. The Melian women carry hidden swords to a Carian banquet, and the wives of the Pelasgian prisoners in Sparta change clothes with their husbands so the men escape; the exiles later found Lyctus in Crete. Lycian women turn back Bellerophon's flood, with several rationalized versions of the tale; the women of Salmantica smuggle swords out past Hannibal. Shorter cases follow: Milesian maidens stopped from suicide by fear of disgrace after death, the orderly courtships of Ceos, and the women of Amphissa who guard the sleeping Thyads.","ideas":["Women's shame and daring rally or rescue their men","Women's fairness makes them trusted judges among the Celts","Fear of dishonour can outweigh even the fear of death"],"terms":[{"t":"Thyads","d":"Women devotees of Dionysus at Delphi, protected by the women of Amphissa."}]},{"i":1179,"j":1194,"loc":"Bravery of Women 14–15 (250–253)","title":"Cloelia's escape and Megisto against Aristotimus","summary":"After Lucretia's death and Tarquin's fall, Roman maidens given as hostages to Porsena swim the Tiber at Cloelia's lead; Rome sends them back, and Porsena honours Cloelia with a horse. In Elis the despot Aristotimus lets a mercenary flog and kill the maiden Micca, imprisons the exiles' wives, and threatens them unless they write to their husbands. Megisto refuses, saying the men should not abandon their country's freedom for their wives' sake, and is barely saved when Aristotimus draws his sword on her. After omens and a dream, Hellanicus and Cylon kill the despot; Megisto then stops the people from outraging his daughters, who die by their own hand with dignity.","ideas":["Keeping faith with an enemy is valued above recovering the hostages","Megisto puts the city's freedom above her own and her children's lives","The liberated must not imitate the cruelty of the despot"],"terms":[]},{"i":1195,"j":1212,"loc":"Bravery of Women 16–19 (253–257)","title":"Pieria, Polycrite, Lampsace and Aretaphila of Cyrene","summary":"Plutarch turns to the deeds of single women, set down in no fixed order. Pieria's wish leads Phrygius to make peace between Miletus and Myus; Polycrite, captive of the Erythraean general Diognetus, sends her brothers word in a cake so the Naxians take his stronghold, and dies of joy at the gate (Aristotle's different version is added). Lampsace warns the Phocaean settlers of her own people's plot and is later worshipped as a goddess. At length, Aretaphila survives torture by the despot Nicocrates, uses her daughter's marriage to turn his brother Leander against him, then traps Leander through the African Anabus; offered a share in government, she withdraws to her loom.","ideas":["A single woman's request or warning can end a war or save a colony","Aretaphila combines endurance under torture with long-planned stratagem","Having freed her city, she refuses all political power"],"terms":[]},{"i":1213,"j":1231,"loc":"Bravery of Women 20–24 (257–260)","title":"Camma, Chiomara, Timocleia and other heroines","summary":"The Galatian priestess Camma, whose husband Sinatus was murdered by Sinorix, pretends to accept Sinorix and poisons him with herself at the altar of Artemis. Stratonice gives her husband Deiotarus an heir through a captive and raises the children as her own; Chiomara has the Roman officer who dishonoured her beheaded and brings the head to her husband. A woman of Pergamum buries the executed Poredorix, and Mithradates, moved, allows it. After Thebes falls, Timocleia lures a Thracian officer down a well and stones him, then answers Alexander so boldly that he frees her and her kin.","ideas":["Revenge is pursued patiently and at the cost of one's own life","Fidelity to a husband shown by deeds rather than words","Courageous speech before a conqueror wins mercy"],"terms":[]},{"i":1232,"j":1246,"loc":"Bravery of Women 25–27 (260–263)","title":"Eryxo, Xenocrite and the wife of Pythes","summary":"In Cyrene Eryxo feigns consent to marry the usurper Laarchus, and her brother Polyarchus kills him in her chamber; she and her aged mother then go with Polyarchus to Egypt and win Amasis's approval. At Cumae Xenocrite, kept by the despot Aristodemus, helps the conspirators reach him unarmed, after another woman's jibe that he is the only man in Cumae shames the citizens; she asks only to bury him and becomes priestess of Demeter. The wife of Pythes serves her gold-mad husband a banquet of golden food to show that the mines are starving the people, and later governs the city well while he lives out his days in a tomb. The work ends here.","ideas":["Women bring down despots through feigned compliance","One sharp word can shame a whole city into action","Golden food shows that wealth without farming and trades is useless"],"terms":[]},{"i":1247,"j":1299,"loc":"Roman Questions 1–14 (263–267)","title":"Wedding rites, kinship customs and covered heads","summary":"Plutarch poses questions on Roman customs and offers several possible answers to each, usually without choosing. He asks why the bride touches fire and water, why five wedding torches are lit, why men may not enter one shrine of Diana, and why cattle horns hang on the Aventine. He explains why men falsely reported dead enter by the roof, why women kiss kinsmen, and why gifts between husband and wife or in-laws are forbidden. He then discusses why Romans cover the head to worship but uncover before eminent men, why Saturn and Honor are worshipped bareheaded, and why sons cover and daughters uncover at a parent's funeral.","ideas":["Each custom receives several alternative explanations: historical, symbolic, philosophical","Marriage rites are read as symbols of union and shared fortune","Odd numbers are treated as better suited to marriage"],"terms":[{"t":"aition (cause)","d":"The explanation of a custom's origin; Plutarch offers rival aitia in question form."}]},{"i":1300,"j":1334,"loc":"Roman Questions 15–25 (267–270)","title":"Gods, festivals and the Roman calendar","summary":"The questions turn to particular gods and dates: why no blood is offered to Terminus, why slave-women are kept from the shrine of Matuta, why the wealthy tithe to Hercules, and why myrtle is barred from Bona Dea. On the year, Plutarch argues that Numa put January first and that a year beginning after the winter solstice is the most natural. He explains the woodpecker, two-faced Janus, and funeral goods sold at Libitina's shrine, then the Kalends, Nones and Ides as phases of the moon. A long answer on why the day after each is unlucky rejects Livy's story of the Allia and offers explanations from number theory, Themistocles' fable of the Feast-Day, and the need for preparation.","ideas":["Numa is credited with peaceful, philosophic reforms of rite and calendar","The month's three fixed days follow the moon's phases","Odd numbers and the monad are linked with the divine, even numbers with the dead"],"terms":[{"t":"Kalends, Nones, Ides","d":"The three fixed points of the Roman month, tied here to new, first-visible and full moon."},{"t":"monad","d":"The unit, the divine beginning of number, followed by the opposing dyad."}]},{"i":1335,"j":1381,"loc":"Roman Questions 26–39 (270–274)","title":"Mourning, walls, brides and military scruples","summary":"Plutarch asks why women mourn in white, suggesting that white alone is pure and undyed, like the soul freed from the body. He explains why walls are sacred but gates not, from the plough-furrow rite of founding, and why children swear by Hercules out of doors. A group of wedding questions follows: why the bride is lifted over the threshold, says that where he is Gaius she is Gaia, and why Talassio is sung. Then come the Argive figures thrown from the bridge, sons brought to dinners, offerings to the dead in December, Larentia the courtesan and Hercules' dice, the Fenestra gate, unrepaired spoils, Metellus's ban on autumn augury, and why soldiers not enrolled may not strike the enemy.","ideas":["White mourning as a sign of the purity of the departed soul","Many wedding customs are traced to the rape of the Sabines","Killing is lawful only under command and accountability"],"terms":[{"t":"Talassio","d":"The wedding cry, explained from spinning or from the Sabine bride carried off for Talasius."}]},{"i":1382,"j":1427,"loc":"Roman Questions 40–50 (274–276)","title":"The Flamen Dialis, Saturn's temple and coinage","summary":"Plutarch asks why the priest of Jupiter may not anoint himself in the open air, and distinguishes rules binding the priest alone from rules taught to all through him; he adds the Roman view that Greek gymnasia made the Greeks idle and soft. He explains the Janus-and-prow coinage, why the temple of Saturn holds the treasury and why ambassadors register there. Further questions concern why the Flamen may not swear an oath, the wine poured out at the Veneralia, the ever-open temple of Horta, Vulcan's temple outside the city, the Consualia, candidates canvassing without tunics, and why the Flamen resigns when his wife dies.","ideas":["Priestly rules can be lessons meant for all citizens","Saturn's reign of justice and harvest suits him as guardian of the treasury","A curse is ill-omened, so a priest of prayer must not swear"],"terms":[{"t":"Flamen Dialis","d":"The priest of Jupiter, bound by many prohibitions discussed throughout the work."}]},{"i":1428,"j":1484,"loc":"Roman Questions 51–69 (276–280)","title":"Lares, festivals, names and minor rites","summary":"Plutarch explains the dog beside the Lares praestites, the bitch sacrificed to Geneta Mana with a veiled prayer that none of the household become good (that is, die), and the mock sale of Sardians at the Capitoline games. He gives derivations for macella, the flute-players' women's dress on the Ides of January, the founding of Carmenta's temple, milk offered to Rumina, and conscript fathers. Further questions treat the shared altar of Hercules and the Muses, the secret name of Rome's guardian god, the pater patratus, the rex sacrorum barred from office, the table never cleared empty, darkness on the wedding night, the Flaminian circus, lictors, the Luperci's dog, and the Septimontium.","ideas":["Rome hides its guardian god's name so enemies cannot call him away","The rex sacrorum keeps kingship only for the gods' sake","Many names are explained by Greek or Latin etymology"],"terms":[{"t":"Lares praestites","d":"Guardian spirits who stand before the house, shown with a dog."},{"t":"rex sacrorum","d":"The king of the sacred rites, kept after the kings' expulsion but denied office."}]},{"i":1485,"j":1531,"loc":"Roman Questions 70–81 (280–283)","title":"Symbols, augury, Fortune and the tribune","summary":"Plutarch explains the furcifer or fork-bearer and the hay on a vicious bull's horn, applied in a saying to Crassus. He reads the augurs' open lanterns and the bar on augurs with sores in Pythagorean fashion, as symbols of a clear and untroubled soul. Questions follow on Servius's shrine of Little Fortune, letting lamps burn out, crescents on noble shoes, the year as Jupiter's and the months as Juno's, why left-hand birds are lucky, and honours for triumphing generals. A longer answer explains that the tribune wears no purple border because his office checks magistracy and must stay humble, open and accessible to all.","ideas":["Small rites read as symbols of moral lessons","Slight turns of Fortune decide the greatest affairs","The tribune's power grows from his humility and accessibility"],"terms":[{"t":"furcifer","d":"Fork-bearer: a slave made to carry a cart-prop through the neighbourhood as a mark of guilt."},{"t":"tribune","d":"An officer of the people whose role is to block magistrates rather than to be one."}]},{"i":1532,"j":1598,"loc":"Roman Questions 82–99 (283–287)","title":"Fasces, human sacrifice, day's beginning and augurs","summary":"Plutarch explains the rods and axes of the fasces, then the paradox that Rome punished a tribe for human sacrifice though it had itself buried alive two Greeks and two Gauls on Sibylline command after the Vestals' trial. He argues that the day is best reckoned from midnight, since sunrise and sunset give no exact boundary. Shorter questions treat wives not grinding grain, no weddings in May, the bride's hair parted with a spear, the Lucar, the Feast of Fools, Hercules' rites, patricians barred from the Capitoline, the oak crown, vultures in augury, Aesculapius outside the city, abstinence from legumes, the burial alive of unchaste Vestals, the October horse, the censors' geese, and why augurs keep office for life.","ideas":["Rods correct curable wrongdoing, axes cut off the incurable","Midnight is chosen as the natural boundary of the day","The vulture as the most righteous and reliable bird of omen","An augur's office is a skill that cannot be voted away"],"terms":[{"t":"fasces","d":"The bundled rods and axes of magistrates, read as a symbol of restraint in punishment."}]},{"i":1599,"j":1647,"loc":"Roman Questions 100–113 (287–291)","title":"Bullae, naming days and the Flamen's prohibitions","summary":"Plutarch asks why slaves keep holiday on the Ides of August, why children wear the bulla, and why girls are named on the eighth day and boys on the ninth, with Pythagorean arguments about odd and even numbers. He corrects the Greek reading of spurii as an abbreviation for sine patre, and explains Liber Pater, widows marrying on holidays, Fortuna Primigenia, histriones, and why Romans avoid marrying close kin. The last questions explain the Flamen Dialis's bans on flour, yeast, raw flesh, the dog, the goat, ivy and vines as symbols of purity and of his role as a refuge for suppliants. The section ends asking why priests may hold no office, suggesting that judging and condemning citizens is at odds with sacrifice.","ideas":["Odd numbers are treated as male and even as female","The Flamen embodies the god and must remain a refuge for suppliants","Priestly duties and the harsh duties of office cannot be combined"],"terms":[{"t":"bulla","d":"The amulet worn by freeborn Roman children, with several explanations offered."},{"t":"spurius","d":"A child of unknown father, explained from the abbreviation Sp. for sine patre."}]},{"i":1648,"j":1698,"loc":"Greek Questions 1–19","title":"Greek Questions: offices, rites and odd names","summary":"Plutarch opens a series of short questions and answers on puzzling Greek customs, titles and phrases, often citing Aristotle, Theophrastus and local historians. He explains civic offices such as the Epidaurian 'dusty-feet', the Cnidian Forgetful Ones and the Delphian Holy Ones, and shaming customs like the Cumaean 'donkey-rider'. Longer answers argue that the Delphian month Bysios means the month of oracular inquiry, and tell how the Charilla festival re-enacts a king's cruelty to an orphan girl who hanged herself. Other answers trace names to migrations and foundation legends: the Aenianians' 'beggar's meat', Neoptolemus' judgement on Odysseus, the Locrians' 'wooden dog', and the riddling oracle about Anthedon.","ideas":["Odd local names and titles preserve the memory of old offices, crimes and migrations","The Delphian festivals Septerion, Heroïs and Charilla as re-enactments of myth and atonement","Oracles fulfilled through trickery or wordplay (Temon's clod of earth, the dog-brier)","Plutarch weighs rival explanations from his sources rather than giving only one"],"terms":[{"t":"Aetia (causes)","d":"The form of the work: each entry asks why a custom or name exists and answers with its origin story."},{"t":"Pythia (prophetic priestess)","d":"Apollo's priestess at Delphi, who once gave oracles only on the seventh of Bysios."}]},{"i":1699,"j":1739,"loc":"Greek Questions 20–37","title":"Greek Questions: oaths, colonies and hero-shrines","summary":"The questions continue with phrases and customs rooted in war and settlement: the Prienian oath by 'the darkness by the Oak', the Cretan 'burners' who buried the dead, the Chalcidian Children's Tomb, and the Andrian curse on the Parians at the Beach of Araenus. Several answers explain bans at shrines, such as why no herald enters the shrine of Ocridion on Rhodes and why Achilles may not be named in the shrine of Tenes. Others concern civic practice, like the Epidamnian 'Seller' who handled trade with the Illyrians and the Milesian 'Perpetual Sailors' who deliberated at sea. The section ends with the Elean hymn calling Dionysus 'with the foot of a bull', for which several explanations are offered, and the story of why Tanagra has a precinct of Achilles.","ideas":["Disasters and wrongs survive as oaths, curses and prohibitions","Land gained by symbolic acts: earth taken from children, a spear thrown into a gate","Shrine bans recall a betrayal (the herald who carried off Cydippê; the flute-player's false witness)","Plutarch often answers with a string of alternative 'Or is it because…' suggestions"],"terms":[{"t":"Thesmophoria","d":"A women's festival of Demeter; the Eretrian women cook its meat by sunlight, which Plutarch links to Agamemnon's captives."},{"t":"Proverb","d":"Several entries explain sayings, such as Pyrrhias, the only man to slay an ox for his benefactor."}]},{"i":1740,"j":1788,"loc":"Greek Questions 38–59","title":"Greek Questions: rituals of blood and expiation","summary":"The final questions include the Minyads' madness and the Agrionia pursuit at Orchomenus, in which a priest of Plutarch's own time killed a woman and died for it. Plutarch weighs explanations for the Arcadian law on entering the Lycaeon and the claim that the trespasser casts no shadow, and tells the story of Eunostus, whose grove women may not enter. Shorter answers explain the Labrandean Zeus' axe from Hippolytê's weapon, the Chalcedonian women's half-veil after a military disaster, the Samian Hall of Fetters, and the Coan priest of Heracles who wears women's dress. The collection ends with the Megarian 'Wagon-rollers', descendants of men punished for drowning a sacred mission during the unbridled democracy.","ideas":["Ritual pursuit and its consequences at Orchomenus, in Plutarch's own lifetime","The Lycaeon: no-shadow legend tested with natural, Pythagorean and legal explanations","Customs born of defeat or scarcity (Chalcedonian veils, Aeginetan 'solitary eaters')","Political violence remembered in names (Hall of Fetters, Wagon-rollers)"],"terms":[{"t":"labrys","d":"The Lydian word for axe, which Plutarch gives as the source of the title Labrandeus for Carian Zeus."},{"t":"Amphictyonic Assembly","d":"The league council that punished the Megarians for the outrage against a sacred mission."}]},{"i":1789,"j":1834,"loc":"Parallela minora 0–21","title":"Parallel stories: Greek and Roman heroism","summary":"The preface says that ancient tales seem like myths, so the author pairs each Greek event with a similar Roman one and names his authorities. The pairs include Cynegeirus at Marathon and a Roman who lost his hands seizing a ship, Agesilaüs at Xerxes' altar and Mucius Scaevola, Othryades' blood-written trophy and Postumius, and Anchurus and Curtius leaping into a chasm. Later pairs set fathers killing traitorous or disobedient sons, maidens saved from sacrifice, Tarpeia beside Demonicê, the Horatii beside Tegean triplets, and Codrus beside Decius. Many Roman parallels are credited to Aristeides the Milesian and other little-known historians, and the section ends with two hunters' wives killed by their husbands' dogs.","ideas":["The stated aim: show ancient wonders are credible by matching them with Roman history","Patterns of self-sacrifice, mutilation and endurance for the city","Parents who punish their own children for treason or disobedience","Each pair closes with a cited source and book number"],"terms":[{"t":"Parallela minora","d":"The Latin title of this collection of paired short Greek and Roman stories."},{"t":"Aristeides the Milesian","d":"The author most often cited for the Roman parallels."}]},{"i":1835,"j":1874,"loc":"Parallela minora 22–41","title":"Parallel stories: crimes, loves and foundings","summary":"The second half pairs stories of forbidden love and family crime: Smyrna and Valeria Tusculanaria, Phaedra and the stepmother Gidica, Macareus and Papirius Romanus, and the murder of Chrysippus matched by that of Firmus. Other pairs set betrayals by love-struck princesses (Callirhoê, Bisaltia), treacherous kinsmen punished (Polymestor, Valerius Gestius), and slave women saving their cities in place of free wives. Founders are matched as well: the wolf-suckled twins of Phylonomê beside Romulus and Remus, Romulus' secret murder in the Senate beside Peisistratus of Orchomenus, and Elaeüs beside Praeneste. The collection ends with the cruel inventions of Perillus and Arruntius Paterculus, river-leaps by Evenus and Annius, and oracles of dancing rustics.","ideas":["Myths of incest and stepmother passion given Roman counterparts","Divine rescue of a maiden by an eagle snatching the sword","Founding legends: wolf-nurse, rivers renamed, cities named from omens","Tyrants who for once act justly by punishing the inventor of a torture"],"terms":[{"t":"Eleutheria","d":"A Smyrnaean festival in which maid-servants wear free women's ornaments, matched by a Roman servants' festival."}]},{"i":1875,"j":1901,"loc":"De fortuna Romanorum 1–8","title":"Fortune and Virtue contend over Rome","summary":"Plutarch stages a contest between Virtue and Fortune over which of them made Roman power, suggesting that for so great a work they may have joined forces, as Plato's middle elements unite fire and earth. He pictures Virtue advancing with her wounded heroes, among them Mucius Scaevola and Horatius, and Fortune arriving swiftly, having shed her wings and globe to stay in Rome, with Numa, Aemilius Paulus, Metellus and Sulla Felix in her train. As witnesses for Fortune he cites Rome's many ancient shrines of Fortune against its late, few shrines of Virtue, Caesar's words to the pilot about 'Caesar's Fortune', and Augustus' prayer and rise over his rivals. He then begins with Romulus: Fortune preserved the exposed twins through the she-wolf and woodpecker, while Virtue only later made Romulus great.","ideas":["Rome as the joint work of Fortune and Virtue, like a cosmos formed from opposing elements","Fortune pictured laying aside wings and globe to remain at Rome","Roman shrines and Roman leaders testify for Fortune","Fortune laid the foundations of Romulus; Virtue finished the building"],"terms":[{"t":"Tychē / Fortuna (Fortune)","d":"Chance or good luck, personified here as one party in the contest."},{"t":"Aretē (Virtue)","d":"Excellence of character and deed, the rival claimant to Rome's greatness."},{"t":"Ruminalis","d":"The wild fig-tree named from ruma, the teat the wolf offered the twins."}]},{"i":1902,"j":1923,"loc":"De fortuna Romanorum 9–13","title":"Fortune's hand from Numa to the Gauls","summary":"Plutarch argues that Fortune gave Numa his long peace, letting the young city set firm like a newly built ship, and notes that Janus' temple was shut under him. Servius Tullius is shown as Fortune's devotee, who founded many shrines of Fortune and rose from a captive's son to king against his own wishes. In the wars, Rome's guardian spirit sent a steady wind, keeping enemies such as the Cimbri and Teutons, Philip and Antiochus apart, and in the Gallic siege the sacred geese woke the Capitol when even the dogs slept. The essay ends, unfinished, with Fortune's kindness in Alexander's early death, which spared Rome a clash with him.","ideas":["Numa's peace as a time for Rome to take root, granted by Fortune","Servius' many named shrines of Fortune and the signs of his fiery birth","Rome's enemies kept apart so they could be fought one at a time","Pontius' climb to the Capitol and the geese that saved it"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (guardian spirit)","d":"Rome's protecting genius, described as a fair wind that never failed the city."},{"t":"Fortuna Primigenia","d":"'First-born' Fortune, one of the shrines Plutarch says Servius founded."}]},{"i":1924,"j":1937,"loc":"De Alexandri fortuna 1.1–8","title":"Alexander as philosopher, not Fortune's favourite","summary":"Replying to the claim that Alexander was Fortune's handiwork, Plutarch makes Alexander list his wounds and the hardships and slim resources with which he began. He argues that Alexander's real equipment came from philosophy and Aristotle, and that a philosopher is judged by words, life and teaching, not writings. Alexander's 'pupils' are compared with Plato's and Socrates': he civilized whole peoples and founded more than seventy cities, putting into practice Zeno's ideal of one community for all men. The mass marriage at Susa and his mixed Persian and Macedonian dress are presented as wise means of uniting the conquered under one law.","ideas":["Alexander's many wounds as proof that he did not owe his empire to Fortune","Philosophy judged by practice: Alexander civilized nations more than any school","Zeno's world-commonwealth realized by Alexander's blending of peoples","Adopting local dress to win the souls of the conquered"],"terms":[{"t":"Zeno's Republic","d":"The Stoic founder's picture of all men as one polity, which Plutarch says Alexander made real."},{"t":"Philosophia (philosophy)","d":"Treated here as a practical way of life shown in deeds, not only theory."}]},{"i":1938,"j":1955,"loc":"De Alexandri fortuna 1.9–12","title":"Alexander's sayings and deeds reveal a philosopher","summary":"Plutarch turns to Alexander's sayings, contrasting them with the self-revealing words of Antigonus, Dionysius and Sardanapalus. He recounts Alexander refusing the Olympic race unless kings competed, comforting the lamed Philip, preferring Achilles' lyre to Paris', and saying 'If I were not Alexander, I should be Diogenes', which Plutarch glosses as a vow to civilize the world. Alexander's deeds are then shown to combine all the virtues, in line with a Stoic principle: his treatment of Porus, Roxanê and the dead Darius, and his sealing of Hephaestion's lips each earn the refrain 'Like a philosopher!'. The first oration ends by comparing him with Socrates and Xenocrates and noting how philosophy steadies judgement in danger.","ideas":["Sayings reveal character; stripped of royal trappings, Alexander's are a philosopher's","His gifts to and respect for philosophers (Xenocrates, Pyrrhon, Diogenes)","Every act of the wise man draws on all the virtues","Philosophy keeps judgement firm against fear"],"terms":[{"t":"Gymnosophists","d":"Indian holy men who live without possessions, mentioned in Alexander's reply to Diogenes."},{"t":"Unity of the virtues (Stoic principle)","d":"The Stoic principle, cited here, that the wise man acts with all the virtues at once."}]},{"i":1956,"j":1982,"loc":"De Alexandri fortuna 2.1–3","title":"Alexander's patronage of the arts","summary":"The second oration notes that Alexander's age produced great artists, and argues this was due to his generous judgement as patron. Plutarch contrasts stingy or jealous rulers such as Dionysius, Alexander of Pherae, Archelaüs, Ateas and Philip with Alexander, who honoured the arts without trying to rival them. Anecdotes follow about the actors Thettalus and Athenodorus, the harper Aristonicus who died fighting, Apelles' 'Wielder of the Thunderbolt', Lysippus' statues, and Stasicrates' offer to carve Mount Athos into his likeness, which Alexander declined. Plutarch then argues that as no great statue is made by Fortune without art, no great man is made by Fortune without Virtue, and contrasts Semiramis with Sardanapalus.","ideas":["A king's favour makes the arts flourish, his envy kills them","Alexander as judge and rewarder of art, not a rival performer","His deeds, not Mount Athos, would be his monument","Wealth and arms without courage only expose weakness"],"terms":[{"t":"Technē (art, craft)","d":"Skill needed to shape material, used as an analogy for Virtue shaping a man."}]},{"i":1983,"j":2004,"loc":"De Alexandri fortuna 2.4–8","title":"Greatness lies in the use of power","summary":"Plutarch argues that Fortune was made glorious by Alexander, and that after his death his empire fell apart like a body without its soul. Greatness lies in using good things well: anyone may inherit a throne, as Aridaeus did, but only virtue can bear power without being puffed up, unlike Cleitus, Demetrius, Lysimachus and Dionysius with their divine titles. Alexander's temperance in habits, marriages and his treatment of Darius' wife won even Darius' admiration and prayer that Alexander should succeed him. Plutarch contrasts the lapses of Tarrias, Antigenes and Philotas, and closes with examples of kings truly made by Fortune, Aegon of Argos and the gardener Abdalonymus of Paphos.","ideas":["Without Alexander, Fortune's gifts produced only squabbling successors","Lesser rulers grew arrogant from small gains; Alexander stayed sober in power","Darius' defeat in virtue as the true defeat","Kings chosen by chance (an eagle, a garden) show what Fortune alone can do"],"terms":[{"t":"Sōphrosynē (self-control)","d":"The temperance Plutarch says was Alexander's own and no gift of Fortune."}]},{"i":2005,"j":2026,"loc":"De Alexandri fortuna 2.9–13e","title":"Fortune as Alexander's adversary","summary":"Plutarch lists the labours and wounds by which Alexander won his greatness and imagines a rebuke to Fortune, who never smoothed his path as she did for kings like Antiochus or Ptolemy Philadelphus. He argues that Fortune opposed Alexander as she did Heracles, distracting him with wars against Illyrians, Triballians and Thebes, while Virtue set him the labour of uniting mankind under one law. He then compares Alexander favourably with Cyrus, Pericles, Agamemnon, Achilles, Diomedes, Solon and Brasidas. The section ends with the leap into the Mallian town, where Fortune isolated him and Virtue, through Limnaeus, Ptolemy and Leonnatus, defended him while an arrow lodged in his chest.","ideas":["Alexander's wounds and hardships as a rebuttal of Fortune's claim","Fortune as a jealous foe, Virtue as the giver of his true labour","Alexander as the sum of the virtues of earlier great men","The Mallian town as the clearest contest of Fortune and Virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"Seisachtheia (relief from burdens)","d":"Solon's cancellation of debts, compared with Alexander paying his soldiers' debts."}]},{"i":2027,"j":2047,"loc":"Were the Athenians more famous in war or in wisdom? 1–4 (345–348)","title":"Men of action outrank historians, painters and poets","summary":"The essay opens (after a lost beginning) by arguing that without generals' deeds there would be no historians: remove Pericles, Nicias, Demosthenes and the rest and Thucydides vanishes. Historians are like actors exhibiting other men's exploits, borrowing a reflected glory. Plutarch praises Athenian painters such as Apollodorus and Euphranor, retells Euphranor's subject, the cavalry battle at Mantineia against Epameinondas, and insists the painter cannot be preferred to the general. Citing Simonides, he treats history as vivid word-painting, so historians too rank below the doers; the Marathon messenger shows that the reporter deserves less than the fighter. Poetry, where myth is an image of a tale that is itself an image of fact, stands still further from the deed.","ideas":["Writers depend on and borrow glory from men of action","Euphranor's painting of the Mantineia cavalry battle cannot rival the victory itself","Painting is silent poetry, poetry speaking painting; vivid history is like painting","Myth is an image of an image, furthest from real deeds"],"terms":[{"t":"enargeia (vividness)","d":"Pictorial vividness by which a writer such as Thucydides makes the reader a spectator of events."},{"t":"mythos (myth)","d":"A false tale resembling a true one, which Corinna tells Pindar is the proper material of poetry."}]},{"i":2048,"j":2069,"loc":"Were the Athenians more famous in war or in wisdom? 5–8 (348–351)","title":"Poets' procession against the generals; orators shamed","summary":"Athens had no great epic or lyric poets, and comedy was thought unworthy, but tragedy flourished as a pleasing deception, as Gorgias put it. Plutarch stages two processions: the poets with their masks, actors and costly choruses, whose expense exceeded the cost of wars for liberty, and the generals bearing cities, islands, temples and the victories Athens still celebrates on fixed festival days. Pindar called Athens the mainstay of Greece for Artemisium and Salamis, not for tragedies. Turning to the orators, he contrasts their speeches with real deeds and mocks Isocrates, who spent nearly twelve years polishing the Panegyric while Athenian commanders were winning battles. The surviving text breaks off comparing Demosthenes' speeches with the deeds of the general of that name.","ideas":["Tragedy is a deception in which the deceived is wiser","Athens spent more on staging plays than on wars for freedom","The city's festivals commemorate battles, not dramatic victories","Isocrates fussed over clashing vowels while others fought"],"terms":[{"t":"chorēgos (chorus-sponsor)","d":"The wealthy citizen who paid for a chorus; Plutarch says the victor's tripod marked a wasted fortune."}]},{"i":2070,"j":2093,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 1–11 (351–355)","title":"Seeking truth about the gods; priestly customs","summary":"Addressing Clea, a priestess at Delphi, Plutarch says the search for truth about the gods is the most holy of works, since the divine is blessed through knowledge; he derives Isis's name from knowing and Typhon's from conceit. The true votary of Isis is not marked by linen and a shaved head but uses reason on the rites. He then explains priestly observances: linen instead of wool, avoidance of surplus matter, abstinence from salt, wine, certain fish, onions and pork, and the separate well of the Apis. Egyptian wisdom is enigmatic, as the sphinxes, the veiled statue at Saïs and the name Amoun show, and Greek sages, especially Pythagoras, borrowed its symbolic manner; so Clea must not take the Egyptian tales of the gods literally.","ideas":["Knowledge of the gods is the highest gift and goal of worship","Purity rules reject bodily surplus such as wool and hair","Egyptian teaching is veiled in riddle and symbol","Pythagorean precepts resemble hieroglyphs"],"terms":[{"t":"Iseion","d":"Isis's shrine, whose name Plutarch links to knowing what truly is."},{"t":"deisidaimonia (superstition)","d":"Irrational fear about the gods, which reasoned interpretation of the rites is meant to avoid."}]},{"i":2094,"j":2111,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 12–20 (355–359)","title":"The myth of Osiris, Isis and Typhon","summary":"Plutarch tells the myth briefly. Hermes wins five extra days from the moon, on which Osiris, Arueris, Typhon, Isis and Nephthys are born. Osiris civilizes Egypt and the world; Typhon and seventy-two conspirators trap him in a chest that floats to Byblus and is enclosed in a tree, where Isis, serving as nurse to the queen's child, recovers it. Typhon later finds the body and cuts it into fourteen pieces; Isis gathers all but the phallus, and Horus, trained by Osiris, defeats Typhon, whom Isis releases. Plutarch adds that such stories are not idle fictions but reflections of some truth, like a rainbow, and points to rites, temples and the many tombs of Osiris.","ideas":["Myths must be read as pointers to truth, not as events","Osiris's death in the chest and dismemberment by Typhon","Isis's search at Byblus and recovery of the body","Horus's victory and Isis's release of Typhon"],"terms":[{"t":"Typhon","d":"The Greek name for Osiris's hostile brother, who kills and dismembers him."},{"t":"Harpocrates","d":"The weak, premature child Isis bears to Osiris after his death."}]},{"i":2112,"j":2140,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 21–29 (359–362)","title":"Not men, but daemons; the origin of Serapis","summary":"Plutarch reports the tombs of Osiris and the priests' claim that the gods' souls shine as stars, Isis as Sothis. He rejects the view that the gods were once kings, condemning Euhemerus for spreading atheism with invented inscriptions, and notes that kings who claimed divinity were soon exposed. Better, he says, is the view that the story concerns daemons, beings stronger than men but subject to passion, as Plato, Xenocrates, Hesiod and Empedocles describe; Isis and Osiris were raised from good daemons to gods. He then tells how Ptolemy Soter brought a statue of Pluto from Sinopê that was named Serapis, and weighs etymologies of the name.","ideas":["Euhemerism turns gods into dead generals and breeds atheism","Daemons stand between gods and men and vary in virtue","Isis and Osiris were promoted from daemons to gods","Serapis is identified with Pluto and with Osiris"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (demigod)","d":"An intermediate being mixing divine nature with soul and passion, carrying prayers up and gifts down."},{"t":"Euhemerus","d":"Writer who claimed the gods were ancient kings, which Plutarch treats as fraud and atheism."}]},{"i":2141,"j":2157,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 30–37 (362–365)","title":"Typhon's rites; Osiris as the Nile and moisture","summary":"Plutarch describes how Egyptians placate and humiliate Typhon: abuse of red-haired men, hatred of the ass, sacrifice of red cattle, and the nickname 'the Ass' for Ochus. He then begins the physical interpretations: some say Osiris is the Nile, Isis the earth and Typhon the sea, which explains the priests' avoidance of salt and sea; the wiser priests make Osiris the whole principle of moisture and Typhon everything dry and fiery. He argues from Bacchic features of the Apis burial and other rites that Osiris is Dionysus, lord of all moisture, and reads the lost phallus and triple-phallic image as the generative power of moisture.","ideas":["Typhon is placated and insulted through animals and colours associated with him","Osiris as the Nile and generative moisture, Typhon as the sea and dryness","Osiris is the same as Dionysus","Moisture is the source of earth, air and fire"],"terms":[{"t":"Osiris","d":"Here read physically as the creative principle of moisture and seed."},{"t":"Apis","d":"The sacred bull of Memphis, called the bodily image of Osiris's soul."}]},{"i":2158,"j":2176,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 38–45 (365–369)","title":"Nile, moon and eclipse readings; two opposed principles","summary":"Isis is read as the land the Nile floods, Horus as the tempered air, Nephthys as the barren borderland, and Typhon's plot as drought and south winds; the rites of Athyr mourn the Nile's retreat and celebrate its finding. Typhon is checked but not destroyed, as the sea once covering Egypt gave way to Nile silt. Astronomical interpreters make Osiris the moon and Typhon the sun, linking seventeen, twenty-eight and fourteen to lunar phases, the Nile's cubits and eclipses, and Anubis to the horizon. Plutarch concludes that all these accounts are partly right, and that the world's mixture of good and evil requires two opposed principles, not one ruling Reason or inanimate atoms.","ideas":["Osiris's seasonal disappearance mirrors the Nile's recession","Egypt was once sea, pushed back by the river","Lunar numbers and eclipses read into the myth","Good cannot cause evil, so nature has two sources"],"terms":[{"t":"Nephthys (Finality)","d":"The outermost, barren land bordering mountains and sea, wife of Typhon."},{"t":"Anubis","d":"Read as the horizon dividing the visible from the unseen."}]},{"i":2177,"j":2192,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 46–52 (369–372)","title":"Dualism in Persians, Greeks and Plato","summary":"Plutarch surveys belief in two powers: Zoroaster's Oromazes and Areimanius with Mithras between, their rival creations and a final destruction of evil; the Chaldaean planets; Heracleitus on strife; Empedocles' love and strife; the Pythagorean opposites; and Plato's Laws, with its beneficent and opposing world-souls and a third nature yearning for the better. He then states his own view: the better power prevails but evil cannot be eradicated; Osiris is reason and order in soul and cosmos, Typhon the irrational and disorderly. He explains Typhonian names and animals, the eye, sceptre and hawk of Osiris, rejects identifying Typhon with the sun, and describes solar rites.","ideas":["Zoroaster's two warring powers and the final victory of good","Plato's Laws posits at least two world-souls, good and opposed","Osiris is order and reason; Typhon disorder and passion","Typhon is not the sun"],"terms":[{"t":"Oromazes and Areimanius","d":"The Persian good god of light and the evil daemon of darkness."},{"t":"Seth","d":"Egyptian name of Typhon, glossed as overpowering and hindering."}]},{"i":2193,"j":2211,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 53–63 (372–376)","title":"Isis as receptive matter yearning for the Good","summary":"Plutarch gives his Platonic reading: Isis is the female, all-receptive principle of nature, Plato's nurse, who loves the first Good and turns from evil. Osiris's soul is eternal while Typhon dismembers his body, the images of being in matter; Horus is the perceptible world, judged legitimate when Hermes, reason, testifies. The three-four-five triangle maps Osiris, Isis and Horus, and Hesiod and Plato's myth of Poverty and Plenty in the Symposium are aligned with the story. Matter is not inert but desires the Good; etymologies of Isis, Osiris and Anubis, the loadstone, and the sistrum symbolise motion opposing Typhonian arrest.","ideas":["Isis is matter that welcomes form and desires the Good","Typhon destroys the images of being in the world of change","The 3-4-5 triangle as father, mother and offspring","Motion and generation resist the check of Typhon"],"terms":[{"t":"chōra / tithēnē (receptacle, nurse)","d":"Plato's names for the receptive principle, here identified with Isis."},{"t":"sistrum","d":"The rattle whose shaking signifies that all things must be kept in motion."}]},{"i":2212,"j":2224,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 64–71 (376–379)","title":"Against reducing gods to crops and things","summary":"Plutarch sums up that the gods are not water, sun or earth themselves, but whatever is orderly in them belongs to Isis and Osiris and whatever is excessive to Typhon. He warns those who read the myth as seasons and sowing not to confine the gods to Egypt or dissolve them into winds and harvests, as calling wine Dionysus would. The gods are common to all peoples under different names, and philosophy must guide interpretation between superstition and atheism. He explains mournful rites as grief at vanishing crops, cites Xenophanes against lamenting gods, criticises calling statues gods, and turns to the dangers of Egyptian animal worship.","ideas":["Gods are not natural objects but the powers that order them","One Reason and Providence is honoured under many names","Philosophy steers between superstition and atheism","Calling gifts by the names of gods led to confused beliefs"],"terms":[{"t":"logos (reason)","d":"The reasoning of philosophy that must guide the reading of rites and myths."},{"t":"atheotēs (atheism)","d":"The opposite error to superstition, reached by those who flee superstition carelessly."}]},{"i":2225,"j":2241,"loc":"Isis and Osiris 72–80 (379–384)","title":"Sacred animals, robes, Osiris of the dead, incense","summary":"Plutarch rejects tales that the gods hid in animals and records political explanations, including a king who set districts against each other through rival animals, and a war in his own day between Oxyrhynchus and Cynopolis. Some animals are honoured for usefulness, others as symbols, such as the beetle, crocodile and ibis, and living creatures are clearer mirrors of the divine than bronze or stone. The many-coloured robes of Isis signify matter, the single bright robe of Osiris the pure intelligible, seen only once as in mystic vision. Osiris rules the dead not beneath the earth but as leader of freed souls toward ineffable beauty. The essay ends with the daily incense of resin, myrrh and sixteen-ingredient cyphi and its effects on health.","ideas":["Animals honoured for use or as symbols of divine power","Living beings mirror the divine better than lifeless images","Isis's varied robes versus Osiris's single robe of light","Osiris leads released souls to unspeakable beauty"],"terms":[{"t":"epoptikon (epoptic part)","d":"The highest, mystic part of philosophy, a single flash of contact with the pure principle."},{"t":"kyphi","d":"An evening incense of sixteen ingredients that calms the body and clears dreams."}]},{"i":2242,"j":2265,"loc":"The E at Delphi 1–9","title":"Proposed meanings of the Delphic E","summary":"Plutarch, writing to Serapion, recalls a discussion led by Ammonius at Delphi in Nero's time on the letter E dedicated in the temple. Ammonius says Apollo is a philosopher as well as a prophet and sets riddles to provoke inquiry. Lamprias explains the E as the number five of the genuine Wise Men; Nicander the priest says it is 'if', the word of questions and prayers; Theon argues it is the logical 'if' of the conditional, basis of demonstration and prophecy. Eustrophus invites the young Plutarch to defend the E as the number five, and Plutarch praises five as 'Marriage' and 'Nature', linking its self-reproduction to Apollo's unity and Dionysus's changes.","ideas":["Apollo's epithets mark stages of philosophical inquiry","E as five Wise Men, as the 'if' of prayer, as the logical conditional","Five as the union of first even and first odd","Apollo and Dionysus as the god's states of unity and dispersion"],"terms":[{"t":"EI","d":"The name of the letter E, also meaning 'if' and 'thou art'."},{"t":"synēmmenon (conditional)","d":"The 'if...then' proposition that Theon calls the principle of demonstration."},{"t":"pempas (pempad)","d":"The number five, the subject of Plutarch's praise."}]},{"i":2266,"j":2291,"loc":"The E at Delphi 10–21","title":"Five everywhere; Ammonius: 'Thou art'","summary":"Plutarch continues finding five in music's consonances and melodic intervals, Plato's possible worlds and elements, the senses, Homer's division of the world, the kinds of living beings and parts of soul, and Plato's five kinds and five goods. Ammonius then answers that the E is neither number nor particle but an address to the god: to 'Know thyself' we reply 'Thou art'. Mortals have no true being, constantly dying and changing as Heracleitus says, and time cannot hold what is. God alone is, in a timeless now, and is one, as the names Apollo and Phoebus show; changes and dispersals belong to another power, Pluto, not to Apollo. The dialogue ends with E and 'Know thyself' as complementary: worship of eternal being and reminder of mortal weakness.","ideas":["Five consonances, elements, senses and kinds of being","E means 'Thou art', ascribing true being to the god","Mortal life is ceaseless becoming with no abiding self","God is one and timeless; change belongs to another power"],"terms":[{"t":"to on (being)","d":"Eternal, unchanging reality, which belongs only to the god."},{"t":"genesis (becoming)","d":"The flux of mortal existence, never arriving at being."},{"t":"Ei hen ('Thou art one')","d":"An old form of the greeting stressing the god's unity."}]},{"i":2292,"j":2324,"loc":"Oracles at Delphi no longer given in verse 1–4","title":"Delphic tour begins: the blue bronze statues","summary":"Basilocles asks Philinus to report the conversation held while he escorted a young visitor, Diogenianus of Pergamum, around the offerings at Delphi; Philinus lists the company: Theon, the poet Serapion, the geometer Boethus who leans toward Epicurus, and two guides. The visitor admires the dark blue patina of the admirals' bronze statues and asks whether some lost alloying art caused it. Theon dismisses the legends about Corinthian bronze and proposes that the air of Delphi, at once fine and dense, draws rust out of the metal and holds it on the surface. Diogenianus and Theon refine the hypothesis with Aristotle's explanation of why oil shows rust and with Homer's close-woven linen.","ideas":["The dialogue is a reported conversation, framed by Basilocles and Philinus","Two tales of Corinthian bronze are set aside as myths","Delphi's air is held to be both tenuous and dense, explaining the patina","Fineness and density can coexist, as in finely woven cloth"],"terms":[{"t":"Corinthian bronze","d":"A prized alloy whose colour was said to come from an accidental fire or a hidden hoard of gold."}]},{"i":2325,"j":2348,"loc":"Oracles at Delphi no longer given in verse 5–11","title":"Bad oracle verse, chance, and Sibylline prophecy","summary":"Diogenianus wonders why oracles in verse are so poor when Apollo leads the Muses; Serapion would rather revise taste, while Boethus mocks the inference with the tale of Pauson's painting. Serapion praises the Sibyl's unadorned speech; Theon holds that the god supplies only the visions and the prophetess supplies voice and metre. Before the stated question, why the Pythia has stopped using verse, can be pursued, statues that fell or sprouted before disasters prompt Philinus to call the offerings full of divinity, and Boethus answers that chance explains such things. Boethus argues that vague prophecies hit the mark only by accident in infinite time; Serapion replies that oracles naming the how and when, such as those on Agesilaus' lameness and the new island near Thera, cannot be chance.","ideas":["The god gives the impulse; the human prophetess gives the words and metre","Omens on votive offerings against the Epicurean appeal to chance","Boethus: vague prediction fulfilled by chance is not foreknowledge","Serapion: detailed, ordered predictions exclude coincidence"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythia","d":"The priestess through whom Apollo's oracle at Delphi speaks."},{"t":"Sibyl","d":"Legendary prophetess whose verses were said to foretell distant events."}]},{"i":2349,"j":2379,"loc":"Oracles at Delphi no longer given in verse 12–18","title":"Offerings, courtesans, and the Muses' spring","summary":"Frogs and water-snakes on the Corinthians' golden palm lead Serapion to a Stoic reading of the sun fed by moist vapours; Philinus mocks it and distinguishes the sun from Apollo. Philinus explains why the treasury bears the Corinthians' name, and Diogenianus is offended by the dedications of Rhodopis and the golden statue of Phryne. Theon answers that spoils of Greek killing Greek are far more shameful than a courtesan's statue, and he praises Croesus' statue of his baker-woman and other worthy gifts. Diogenianus then insists on the real question; the party sits by the temple of Earth and the Muses' spring, where Boethus notes the oracle's link with verse, and Serapion and Philinus urge seeking causes rather than abandoning belief, since philosophy and astronomy also moved from verse to prose.","ideas":["Symbolic devices on offerings and the sun-versus-Apollo question","Monuments of war between Greeks condemned above a courtesan's image","The Muses guarded the oracle when responses were sung","A change of form is no reason to reject an art"],"terms":[{"t":"ekpyrōsis (Conflagration)","d":"The Stoic doctrine of periodic world-burning, mocked by Philinus here."}]},{"i":2380,"j":2397,"loc":"Oracles at Delphi no longer given in verse 19–23","title":"Theon: the god uses the prophetess as instrument","summary":"Theon shows that many ancient oracles were already in prose, citing responses to the Spartans, to Deinomenes, to Procles of Epidaurus, and Lycurgus' laws. He sets out his principle: soul is God's instrument, and an instrument reproduces its user's thought only mixed with its own nature, as the moon transmits sunlight weakened. The Pythia's soul, stirred by the god, moves by both the divine impulse and its own nature, and an untrained mind cannot be made to speak like a poet; the present priestess is a farmer's daughter with no art. Earlier ages had temperaments readily inclined to poetry, as love makes poets only of those already gifted.","ideas":["Prose oracles existed from early times","Inspiration mixes the god's impulse with the medium's nature","The god signifies, neither speaking nor concealing (Heraclitus)","Inspiration, like love or wine, works on existing aptitudes"],"terms":[{"t":"enthousiasmos (inspiration)","d":"Here a compound of two movements: the god's impulse and the soul's own nature."},{"t":"organon (instrument)","d":"The soul or body as the tool through which a higher power acts, colouring what it transmits."}]},{"i":2398,"j":2419,"loc":"Oracles at Delphi no longer given in verse 24–30","title":"Why plain prose oracles are a change for the better","summary":"Theon argues that language is like currency: once poetry was the common coin, but as life grew simpler, history, philosophy and the oracle dropped metre for clarity. Old ambiguity also protected the god's ministers when powerful states and tyrants consulted him, while verse aided memory for colonists' directions, as with Battus and Lysander. Charlatans' verse brought poetry into disrepute. Now, amid peace and petty questions about marrying or sailing, plain answers suit the truth-loving Pythia, as brief sayings like 'Know Thyself' do; Delphi's new prosperity shows the god's presence, and those who miss riddles are like children preferring rainbows to the sun.","ideas":["Style follows the times, as coinage does","Ambiguity once shielded the oracle's servants from the powerful","Verse served memory when complex instructions were needed","The oracle's present revival attests the god at work"],"terms":[{"t":"Gnōthi sauton (Know Thyself)","d":"Delphic maxim cited by Theon as proof that brief, plain sayings carry dense meaning."}]},{"i":2420,"j":2436,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 1–6 (409–412)","title":"Travellers at Delphi and the failing oracles","summary":"Lamprias, addressing Terentius Priscus, recounts how Demetrius the grammarian, back from Britain, and Cleombrotus of Sparta, back from Egypt and beyond, met at Delphi. Cleombrotus reports the priests' claim that Ammon's ever-burning lamp uses less oil each year, proving years grow shorter; Demetrius ridicules this, and Ammonius shows it would upset all astronomy, suggesting air or the oil itself as causes. Asked about Ammon's oracle, Demetrius turns to the general decline: Boeotia's many oracles, famous in the Persian Wars and recalled through the tales of Mys and Tegyrae, are now silent save Lebadeia. The group enters the Cnidian clubhouse to discuss the cause.","ideas":["Small signs may or may not justify large conclusions","Ammonius refutes the shrinking-year inference","Boeotia's oracles have nearly all fallen silent","The question set: why have oracles become so weak"],"terms":[{"t":"omphalos","d":"The navel-stone at Delphi, held to mark the centre of the earth."}]},{"i":2437,"j":2461,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 7–11 (413–416)","title":"Wickedness, depopulation, and the doctrine of demigods","summary":"The Cynic Planetiades says the wonder is that Delphi still speaks amid human wickedness; Lamprias calms him and he leaves. Ammonius holds that the god, who does nothing in excess, has reduced oracles in proportion to Greece's depopulation, so one priestess now suffices. Lamprias denies that the god abolishes oracles, blaming matter and nature, and rejects the idea that the god speaks through prophets like a ventriloquist. Cleombrotus then proposes the race of demigods between gods and men, traced to Zoroaster, Orpheus, Egypt, and Hesiod's four classes, and argues from Hesiod's verses that they are long-lived but mortal; Demetrius disputes his reading of 'generation'.","ideas":["Oracles reduced to match Greece's shrunken population","The god creates but nature and matter dissolve","Demigods as a mediating class bridge gods and men","Hesiod's lifespans imply demigods eventually die"],"terms":[{"t":"daimones (demigods)","d":"Beings between gods and men, subject to some human emotion and change."},{"t":"genea (generation)","d":"Cleombrotus takes it as one year in Hesiod's verse; Demetrius objects."}]},{"i":2462,"j":2485,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 12–15 (416–418)","title":"Demigods as ministers; oracles fail when they depart","summary":"Cleombrotus rejects the Stoic Conflagration reading and maintains that, whatever their lifespan, beings between gods and men exist and should be honoured. He cites Xenocrates' triangles and the moon as images of their mixed nature, and argues that without them the gods would be remote or dragged into human affairs. Gloomy rites, human sacrifices and myths of gods' exiles belong to demigods, some of them violent, not to gods; the Delphic festival of the Stepterion, with its burnt hut and the boy's flight to Tempe, points to some deed of a demigod rather than a fight with a serpent. He concludes that oracles fall silent when their guardian spirits leave and revive when they return.","ideas":["Equilateral, isosceles and scalene triangles for gods, demigods and men","Demigods guard rites and avenge injustice; some are harsh","The Python myth misreads the Delphic rite","Oracles are like instruments that sound only when their players are present"],"terms":[{"t":"Stepterion","d":"The eight-yearly Delphic rite in which a hut is fired and a boy flees to Tempe for purification."}]},{"i":2486,"j":2496,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 16–20 (418–420)","title":"Can demigods die? Great Pan is dead","summary":"Heracleon accepts demigods as ministers of the oracles but objects to making them sinful and mortal; Cleombrotus replies that without such traits they would not differ from gods. Philip cites Plato, Xenocrates, Chrysippus and Democritus for bad spirits and tells Epitherses' story of the pilot Thamus, ordered from Paxi to announce that Great Pan is dead, followed by a cry of lament, and of Tiberius' inquiry. Demetrius reports Britons' belief that storms mark the passing of mighty souls, and of an island where Cronus sleeps guarded by Briareus. Cleombrotus notes that Stoics make all gods but one perishable and mocks the Epicureans; Ammonius rebuts the Epicurean claim that wicked beings cannot be long-lived.","ideas":["Demigods must differ from gods in mortality or passion","The tale of the death of Great Pan","British islands, departing great souls, and the sleeping Cronus","Long life does not depend on virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"Pan","d":"The god whose death was announced to Thamus; Tiberius' scholars took him for the son of Hermes and Penelope."}]},{"i":2497,"j":2511,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 21–23 (420–423)","title":"The stranger of the Persian Gulf and 183 worlds","summary":"Cleombrotus describes a fragrant-breathed, disease-free sage near the Persian Gulf who meets men once a year and prophesies once a year. The sage taught that the Python story, the Titans and Typhon concern demigods, whose exile to another world dims their cults, and that demigods bear names of the gods they serve. Asked about Plato's link, Cleombrotus reports the sage's 183 worlds set in a triangle around the Plain of Truth, seen by good souls every ten thousand years. Lamprias identifies the doctrine as that of Petron of Himera, a Greek source; Heracleon and Demetrius dispute whether Homer or Plato really posited five worlds, and the company agrees to examine the question briefly.","ideas":["Demigods' battles and exiles explain myths and vanished cults","183 worlds arranged in a triangle about the Plain of Truth","The stranger's doctrine traced to Petron of Himera","Plato's one-world view versus his hint of five"],"terms":[{"t":"Plain of Truth","d":"In the stranger's account, the common hearth holding the patterns of all that happens."}]},{"i":2512,"j":2525,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 24–31 (423–427)","title":"Lamprias argues for many, but not infinite, worlds","summary":"Lamprias argues that the reasons against infinite worlds do not exclude several: God's justice and friendliness need other gods and worlds to act toward, and nature groups things in kinds. Fear that leftover matter would crash into the world is groundless, since each world has its own limited matter. Against Aristotle's physics he argues that 'centre' is relative to bodies, not to void, so each world has its own centre, up and down; he also rebuts Chrysippus' central-place argument and the Stoic worry about many Zeuses, picturing gods visiting world after world like the Dioscuri. Philip then asks why Plato would posit exactly five worlds.","ideas":["Divine virtues imply a society of worlds","Each world has its own centre and natural places","One Providence can govern many worlds","Infinity admits no god, only chance"],"terms":[{"t":"kenon (void)","d":"Empty space beyond the world, which on Lamprias' view has no centre or direction."}]},{"i":2526,"j":2535,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 32–37 (427–431)","title":"Five regular solids, five categories, five worlds","summary":"Lamprias reports Theodorus of Soli's view that the regular solids could not all arise first from the same matter, so matter is divided into five worlds; Ammonius finds the argument self-contradictory and notes the cube cannot transmute into the others. Lamprias offers his own view: the five solids image Plato's five greatest kinds, Being, Identity, Difference, Movement and Rest. He adds that five arises from the One and the indefinite dyad, and lists nature's many fives: senses, zones, circles, notes, intervals. Finally he explains Plato's text through matter's self-division before God ordered it, but keeps the Academy's caution, claiming only that the worlds are many but limited.","ideas":["Theodorus' generative argument and Ammonius' objection","Solids matched to Being, Identity, Difference, Movement, Rest","Five as the first number joining even and odd","Academic reserve on the number of worlds"],"terms":[{"t":"aoristos dyas (indeterminate duality)","d":"The principle of the unlimited and even, checked by the One."},{"t":"megista genē (greatest kinds)","d":"Plato's five: Being, Identity, Difference, Movement and Rest."}]},{"i":2536,"j":2556,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 38–42 (431–434)","title":"The soul's own prophetic power and the vapour","summary":"Demetrius brings the talk back: how do demigods make oracles speak? Ammonius says demigods are souls, which may impress the future on other souls; Lamprias, asked to repeat his Lebadeia discussion, argues that embodied souls already possess this power, dimmed by the body as the sun by fog, as shown by memory. It shows itself in dreams and at death, and is roused by earth's prophetic exhalation, which may work by heat, dryness, chilling or affinity, as particular waters suit particular tasks. The tale of the shepherd Coretas marks the discovery at Delphi; the soul's prophetic power needs the vapour as the eye needs light, and the sun stands to Apollo as light to truth.","ideas":["Prophecy is innate in souls but obscured by the body","Memory as the counterpart of foreknowledge","The prophetic exhalation alters the body's temperament","Eye : light :: prophetic soul : exhalation; sun as Apollo's offspring"],"terms":[{"t":"pneuma (prophetic breath/exhalation)","d":"A vapour from the earth that disposes the soul to inspiration."}]},{"i":2557,"j":2568,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 43–46 (434–435)","title":"Exhalations fail; Ammonius fears a godless account","summary":"Lamprias argues that earth is lasting but its powers move and fail, as springs vanish, mines are exhausted and Euboean asbestos has died out; rains, thunderbolts and earthquakes may quench or shift prophetic vapours, as at Orchomenus. Demetrius tells how the oracle of Mopsus answered a sceptical Cilician ruler's sealed question with one word, 'Black', confounding his Epicureans. Ammonius then objects that tracing prophecy to vapours drives out gods and demigods alike, making sacrifices, the trembling victim and the chaste priestess pointless, and leaving Delphi's benefactions to chance. He asks Lamprias to answer.","ideas":["Natural causes explain the failing of particular oracles","The sealed-tablet test at the oracle of Mopsus","Objection: physical causes make ritual and the priestess pointless","Delphi's benefits should be credited to Providence"],"terms":[{"t":"anathumiasis (exhalation)","d":"Aristotle's school treats it as the cause of changes in the earth."}]},{"i":2569,"j":2581,"loc":"The obsolescence of oracles 47–52 (435–438)","title":"Two causes of prophecy and the stricken priestess","summary":"Lamprias defends himself with Plato: as an artist works through materials, God acts through matter, and explaining the material cause does not deny the agent, as Polygnotus' paintings and tempered steel show. Old theologians named only the higher cause, physicists only the lower; prophecy has the soul as its matter and the exhalation as its plectrum, while earth and sun are gods and demigods oversee the blend. Trembling victims signal the right moment, since the vapour and the priestess's temperament vary, as dreams vary. A recent priestess, forced to prophesy after a reluctant omen, was seized by a baleful spirit and died; Lamprias closes by leaving his views open to objection and postponing the Sun–Apollo question.","ideas":["Agent and material causes both needed, after Plato","Exhalation as plectrum, soul as instrument","Sacrificial omens test the priestess's readiness","Prophetic power is divine but not imperishable"],"terms":[{"t":"aitia (cause)","d":"Distinguished here into the divine agent or purpose and the necessary material cause."}]}]});